  "core": "fake: FakeCore from tests/core_integration.py, a Python thread per connection in the benchmark process; no hc server is started or contacted",
  "method": [
    "warm: every case runs once before measuring; samples are interleaved in a seeded shuffle",
    "init cases: a fresh `bash -i` on a new pty per sample, made session leader with `setsid -c` and registered with the fake core before it reads its rc file; the rc file times `$(exec herdr-revive --host core init bash)` with EPOCHREALTIME, so wall time includes the fork and exec the installed rc line pays",
    "init_relaunch: the snapshot was saved by the hook from a shell running `sleep 600`; the boot journal is deleted before each sample so the same pane relaunches again; each sample checks the printed command and an applied journal entry",
    "init_no_relaunch: auto_save and auto_restore on, no snapshot; prints the hook only",
    "shell_control: the same shell and rc file running `true` instead of herdr-revive",
    "save and autosave cases: herdr-revive invoked directly under GNU time; wall time is measured around the time process, as scripts/benchmark.py does",
    "save_N: one live bash at its prompt plus N-1 more fake core records reusing that shell's pid"
  ],
  "rss": "GNU time maximum RSS. Init cases measure the whole shell tree (setsid, bash, its subshell and herdr-revive), so compare them with shell_control; command cases measure herdr-revive alone.",
  "limitations": [
    "fake core, not hc: real core latency is not included",
    "extra save records share one shell process",
    "no cold-cache claim",
    "host load uncontrolled"
  ],
